# Understanding HTTP Methods

The web runs on **HTTP**, the Hypertext Transfer Protocol. Part of what HTTP standardizes is a set of **methods**: a word attached to every request saying what the client wants done. The same set applies to every application on the web, whatever it does.

The servers we built in [Lesson 1](<01 - Creating a Simple Web Server.webnb>) ignored the method entirely. Every request was treated as a request to fetch something, which was fine, because that is all a browser does when it loads a page.

## The Two You Will Use

**`GET`** asks for a resource. It only retrieves; it is not supposed to change anything. Every HTML file, stylesheet, script, and image your browser loads arrives through a `GET`.

```
GET /users/42        retrieve the user with id 42
```

**`POST`** sends data to the server, and by convention means "create something new".

```
POST /users          create a new user
```

## Two More Worth Recognizing

**`PUT`** also sends data, and means "replace what is there".

```
PUT /users/42        replace user 42 with what I am sending
```

**`DELETE`** asks for something to be removed.

```
DELETE /users/42     remove user 42
```

There are several more methods beyond these four, and they come up rarely enough that MDN's list is the right place to meet them.

Notice the pattern in those examples: the **URL** names the thing, and the **method** says what to do to it. `/users/42` is the same user in all three; `GET`, `PUT`, and `DELETE` are three different intentions about them.

These meanings are conventions, not rules the protocol enforces. Nothing stops you writing a server where `GET /delete-everything` wipes the database. Following the conventions is what makes an interface predictable to anyone else, and browsers and caches assume `GET` is safe to repeat. We will catch the conventions being *merely* conventions, live, in a moment.

## Seeing the Method on the Server

The method arrives on the same `request` object as the URL, as **`request.method`**. This reading's demo is `demo-server/methods-server.js`, sitting next to this notebook, and the first thing it does with every request is log exactly that:

```{walkthrough}
title: demo-server/methods-server.js
file: demo-server/methods-server.js
region: log
@"request.method": One line, and the server can see the client's intention next to its URL. `routing-server.js` has been logging this since Lesson 1, back when every method it printed was `GET`.

step: The file-serving branch
file: demo-server/methods-server.js
region: files
The handler ends the way Lesson 1's static server did, treating the URL as a file name.
@"path.join": The URL becomes a path inside `methods-page/`, the folder holding this reading's demo page.
```

The comment in that second step is the thing to notice: nothing in the file-serving code asks what the method was. Hold that thought.

Run the server (stopping any other server on port 3000 first, with `Ctrl+C` in its terminal):

```
$ cd "demo-server"
$ node methods-server.js
Listening on port 3000
```

Open `http://localhost:3000` and look at the terminal:

```
GET /
GET /styles.css
GET /script.js
```

One page load, three requests, every one a `GET`, which is what to expect: a browser loading a page only ever gets. First the page, then each file the page asks for.

## Choosing the Method in `fetch`

So how does anything other than a `GET` ever get sent? One way is [`fetch`](<../../Course 2/Module 3 - Asynchronous Data and Fetching/06 - fetch.webnb>), which we met in Course 2. On its own, `fetch` sends a `GET`:

```javascript
fetch('/something.txt');            // a GET
```

The optional second argument is an options object, and `method` is one of its properties:

```javascript
fetch('/something.txt', { method: 'POST' });
```

The same works for `'PUT'`, `'DELETE'`, or any other valid method. This is how a page does anything other than read: the browser never sends a `POST` on its own, your JavaScript asks for it.

The page you just loaded has a button for each method, wired up in `demo-server/methods-page/script.js`:

```{walkthrough}
title: demo-server/methods-page/script.js
file: demo-server/methods-page/script.js
region: send
This file is client-side code: the server sends it to the browser like any other file, and the browser runs it. `send` fetches a URL and puts the response's status and text on the page.
@"fetch(url, options)": Whatever options object a button passes lands here, as the second argument to `fetch`.

step: One button per method
file: demo-server/methods-page/script.js
region: method-clicks
@"send('/something.txt');": No options at all, so this request is a `GET`.
@"{ method: 'POST' }": The same URL with the method changed. The next two buttons do the same with `PUT` and `DELETE`.
```

With the server running, click the four buttons in the page's top row and watch two places:

1. The terminal logs one line per click: `GET /something.txt`, `POST /something.txt`, `PUT /something.txt`, `DELETE /something.txt`.
2. The Network tab shows the same requests, with the method in its own column.

Then notice what the page itself shows: all four buttons got `200` and the contents of `something.txt`. Sending `DELETE` deleted nothing, because the file-serving code never checks `request.method`, so no code anywhere acted on the intention. That is the "conventions, not rules" caveat on screen. A method is a request, in both senses of the word.

## Answering a POST

Acting on the method is the server's job, and `methods-server.js` does act on it for one URL. Two `if` conditions check the method and the URL *together*:

```{walkthrough}
title: The /users route
file: demo-server/methods-server.js
region: users
The same URL, `/users`, answered two different ways depending on the method. That is the shape of nearly every web API you will use.
@"request.method === 'GET' && request.url === '/users'": Both conditions together: this branch is for reading the list of users, and only for reading it.
@"writeHead(201": One status code worth adding to Lesson 1's list. A `GET` that succeeds answers `200 OK`; a `POST` that succeeds *by creating something* conventionally answers `201 Created`, which tells the client not just that it worked but that a new resource now exists.
```

The bottom row of buttons on the page sends `GET /users` and `POST /users`. Click them and the page shows the difference: `200 Here is the list of users` for one, `201 User created` for the other. (Nothing is truly created here; a real server would save the new user before claiming `201`.)

## Routing on Method and URL Together

A notebook cell cannot run a real server, but the routing logic is plain JavaScript, so here it is against a stand-in response object:

```{node autorun}
function makeFakeResponse() {
  const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

  sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
    sent.statusCode = code;
    sent.headers = headers;
  };

  sent.end = function (text) {
    sent.body = text;
  };

  return sent;
}

function handleRequest(request, response) {
  if (request.method === 'GET' && request.url === '/users') {
    response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('here is the list of users');
  } else if (request.method === 'POST' && request.url === '/users') {
    response.writeHead(201, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('user created');
  } else {
    response.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('not found');
  }
}

const requests = [
  { method: 'GET', url: '/users' },
  { method: 'POST', url: '/users' },
  { method: 'GET', url: '/nowhere' }
];

for (const request of requests) {
  const response = makeFakeResponse();

  handleRequest(request, response);

  console.log(request.method + ' ' + request.url + ' -> ' + response.statusCode + ' ' + response.body);
}
```

Try adding `{ method: 'DELETE', url: '/users' }` to the list of requests and rerunning the cell: it falls through to the `404`, because the handler checks the method as well as the URL.

From here on we will concentrate on `GET` and `POST`, which is the subject of the next reading.

## Exercises

1. Complete the handler so that reading the list of notes succeeds, creating a note reports that something was created, and anything else is not found.

    ```{node}
    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleRequest(request, response) {
      // TODO:
      //   GET  /notes -> 200, body 'note list'
      //   POST /notes -> 201, body 'note created'
      //   anything else -> 404, body 'not found'
      //   Send Content-Type text/plain every time.
    }

    const listed = makeFakeResponse();
    handleRequest({ method: 'GET', url: '/notes' }, listed);

    console.log(listed.statusCode + ' ' + listed.body);
    ```
    ```+solution
    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleRequest(request, response) {
      if (request.method === 'GET' && request.url === '/notes') {
        response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        response.end('note list');
      } else if (request.method === 'POST' && request.url === '/notes') {
        response.writeHead(201, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        response.end('note created');
      } else {
        response.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        response.end('not found');
      }
    }

    const listed = makeFakeResponse();
    handleRequest({ method: 'GET', url: '/notes' }, listed);

    console.log(listed.statusCode + ' ' + listed.body);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(listed.statusCode + '|' + listed.body).should('equal', '200|note list').run('`GET /notes` should answer `200` with the body `note list`', 'Handled the GET');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleRequest({ method: 'POST', url: '/notes' }, r); return r.statusCode + '|' + r.body; })()).should('equal', '201|note created').run('`POST /notes` should answer `201`, the code for something having been created, with the body `note created`', 'Handled the POST');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleRequest({ method: 'GET', url: '/nowhere' }, r); return r.statusCode + '|' + r.body; })()).should('equal', '404|not found').run('Any other request should answer `404` with the body `not found`', 'Handled the unknown URL');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleRequest({ method: 'DELETE', url: '/notes' }, r); return r.statusCode; })()).should('equal', 404).run('`DELETE /notes` is not one of the two routes, so it should be a `404` too. Check the method as well as the URL', 'Checked the method, not just the URL');
    ```

2. Write the `fetch` call that asks the server to delete the photo at `/photos/42`.

    ```{javascript}
    // Write your fetch call here
    ```
    ```+solution
    fetch('/photos/42', { method: 'DELETE' });
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(/fetch\s*\(\s*['"]\/photos\/42['"]/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Call `fetch` with the URL `/photos/42`', 'Called fetch with the right URL');
    wrap(/\{[^}]*method\s*:\s*['"]DELETE['"][^}]*\}/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Pass `{ method: \'DELETE\' }` as the second argument, since `fetch` sends a `GET` otherwise', 'Set the method');
    ```

3. Picking the method for a removal.

    ```{mcq}
    A music app lets the user remove one of their saved playlists. The client sends the request to `/playlists/8`. Which method says "remove this resource"?
    correct: Correct! `DELETE` is the method whose meaning is removal, and the URL names which playlist. The server still needs code that acts on that intention.
    wrong: The URL already names which playlist. The method has to say what should happen to it.
    [x] `DELETE`
    [ ] `GET`
    [ ] `POST`
    [ ] `PUT`
    ```

4. Reporting that something new exists.

    ```{mcq}
    A recipe site's Save button sends `POST /recipes` carrying a new recipe, and the server stores it successfully. Which status code fits the response?
    correct: Correct! `201 Created` is the conventional answer to a `POST` that creates a resource. `200` would also mean success, and `201` says more.
    wrong: The request succeeded, so it is a 2xx code. Which one is specifically about something having been created?
    [x] `201`
    [ ] `301`
    [ ] `404`
    [ ] `500`
    ```

